Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum of $\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{x_i^\beta}{1-x_i^\alpha}$ subject to $\sum x_i^\alpha = 1$, where $\frac{\alpha}{\beta} = \frac{n-1}{n-2}$.

**Setup.** Let $t_i = x_i^\alpha \in (0,1)$, so $\sum t_i = 1$. Since $\frac{\beta}{\alpha} = \frac{n-2}{n-1}$, we have $x_i^\beta = t_i^{(n-2)/(n-1)}$. Setting $p = \frac{n-2}{n-1}$, the objective becomes:

$$F = \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{t_i^p}{1 - t_i}, \quad \sum t_i = 1,\ t_i \in (0,1).$$

**Claim:** The minimum is $\dfrac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}$, achieved at $t_1 = \cdots = t_n = \frac{1}{n}$.

**Proof via tangent line (supporting line) method.** Define $g(t) = \frac{t^p}{1-t}$ for $t \in (0,1)$. I will show that

$$g(t) \geq \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}\, t \quad \text{for all } t \in (0,1), \tag{$\star$}$$

with equality at $t = 1/n$. Summing $(\star)$ over $i$ then gives $F \geq \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}\sum t_i = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}$, with equality when all $t_i = 1/n$.

**Verification that the line is tangent at $t=1/n$.** At $t = 1/n$:
$$g(1/n) = \frac{(1/n)^p}{(n-1)/n} = \frac{n^{1-p}}{n-1} = \frac{n^{1/(n-1)}}{n-1},$$
so $n\cdot g(1/n) = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}$. The derivative is
$$g'(t) = \frac{t^{p-1}\bigl[p+(1-p)t\bigr]}{(1-t)^2}.$$
At $t=1/n$: $p + (1-p)/n = \frac{n-2}{n-1}+\frac{1}{n(n-1)} = \frac{(n-1)^2}{n(n-1)} = \frac{n-1}{n}$, giving
$$g'(1/n) = n^{1/(n-1)}\cdot\frac{n^2}{(n-1)^2}\cdot\frac{n-1}{n} = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}.$$
Thus the tangent line at $t=1/n$ is $L(t) = g(1/n) + g'(1/n)(t-1/n) = \frac{n^{1/(n-1)}}{n-1}\bigl(1 + nt - 1\bigr) = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}\,t$. ✓

**Proof of $(\star)$.** Since $1-t>0$, inequality $(\star)$ is equivalent to $t^p \geq Ct(1-t)$ where $C = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}$, i.e., $t^{p-1} \geq C(1-t)$. Since $p-1 = -\frac{1}{n-1}$, this becomes $t^{-1/(n-1)} \geq C(1-t)$.

Substitute $t = s^{n-1}$ with $s \in (0,1)$: the inequality becomes $s^{-1} \geq C(1 - s^{n-1})$, i.e., $1 \geq C(s - s^n)$, i.e.,

$$s - s^n \leq \frac{1}{C} = \frac{n-1}{n^{n/(n-1)}} \quad \text{for all } s \in (0,1). \tag{$\star\star$}$$

**Proof of $(\star\star)$.** Let $\psi(s) = s - s^n$ on $(0,1)$. Then $\psi'(s) = 1 - ns^{n-1} = 0$ gives the unique critical point $s_0 = n^{-1/(n-1)} \in (0,1)$. Since $\psi''(s) = -n(n-1)s^{n-2} < 0$, this is a maximum. The maximum value is:

$$\psi(s_0) = n^{-1/(n-1)} - n^{-n/(n-1)} = n^{-1/(n-1)}\!\left(1 - \tfrac{1}{n}\right) = \frac{n-1}{n^{n/(n-1)}}.$$

Therefore $s - s^n \leq \frac{n-1}{n^{n/(n-1)}}$ for all $s \in (0,1)$, with equality at $s = n^{-1/(n-1)}$, i.e., $t = 1/n$. This establishes $(\star\star)$ and hence $(\star)$.

**Conclusion.** Summing $(\star)$:

$$\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{t_i^p}{1-t_i} \geq \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n t_i = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1},$$

with equality when $t_i = 1/n$ for all $i$, i.e., $x_i = n^{-1/\alpha}$ for all $i$. This point is feasible ($\sum x_i^\alpha = n \cdot \frac{1}{n} = 1$) and lies in the interior of the domain, so the minimum is attained.

$$\boxed{\dfrac{n^{\,n/(n-1)}}{n-1}}$$

